Git Commands — Complete Practical Script

#1. What is Git?

#Git is a version control tool. It tracks changes in your code, helps you go back to old versions, create branches, merge work, and collaborate with others.

#GitHub is a cloud platform where Git repositories are stored online.

#2. Basic Git Setup
Check Git version
git --version

#Shows installed Git version.

Set username
git config --global user.name "Gande Saivamshi"
Set email
git config --global user.email "yourmail@gmail.com"
Check config
git config --list

#3. Create / Initialize Repository
Create new folder
mkdir my-project
cd my-project
Initialize Git
git init

#This creates a hidden .git folder. Now Git starts tracking this project.

#4. Check Status
git status

#Shows:

#Untracked files
#Modified files
#Staged files
#Branch name

#5. Add Files to Staging Area

#Add one file
git add index.html

#Add all files
git add .

#Staging means: “prepare these changes for commit.”

#6. Commit Changes
git commit -m "initial commit"

Commit means saving a snapshot of your code.

#Example:

git add .
git commit -m "added homepage"

#7. View Commit History
git log

#Short format:

git log --oneline

#Example output:

a1b2c3d added homepage
e4f5g6h initial commit

#8. Connect Local Repo to GitHub
Add remote repository
git remote add origin https://github.com/username/repo-name.git
Check remote
git remote -v

#Output:

origin  https://github.com/username/repo-name.git (fetch)
origin  https://github.com/username/repo-name.git (push)

#9. Push Code to GitHub

#Push main branch
git branch -M main
git push -u origin main

#After first push, next time simply:

git push

#10. Clone Repository
git clone https://github.com/username/repo-name.git

#This downloads the GitHub repo to your system.

#11. Pull Latest Changes
git pull

#Gets latest changes from GitHub to local system.

#Full command:

git pull origin main

#12. Branch Commands
Check branches
git branch
Create new branch
git branch feature-login
Switch branch
git checkout feature-login

#Modern command:

git switch feature-login

#Create and switch together
git checkout -b feature-login

or

git switch -c feature-login

#13. Merge Branch

#First go to main branch:

git checkout main

#Then merge:

git merge feature-login

#This brings feature-login changes into main.

#14. Delete Branch

#Delete local branch
git branch -d feature-login

#Force delete:

git branch -D feature-login
Delete remote branch
git push origin --delete feature-login

#15. Git Workflow Example
git clone https://github.com/username/project.git
cd project

git checkout -b feature-navbar

# make changes

git status
git add .
git commit -m "added navbar"
git push origin feature-navbar

#Then create Pull Request on GitHub.

#16. Undo Commands

#Unstage file
git restore --staged file.txt

#Discard local changes
git restore file.txt

#Undo last commit but keep changes
git reset --soft HEAD~1

#Undo last commit and remove changes
git reset --hard HEAD~1

#Careful: --hard deletes changes.

#17. Fix Wrong Remote URL

#Check remote:

git remote -v

#Change remote URL:

git remote set-url origin https://github.com/username/new-repo.git

#18. Rename Branch
git branch -M main

#This renames current branch to main.

#19. Common Real-Time Push Commands
#First-time GitHub push
git init
git add .
git commit -m "first commit"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/username/repo.git
git push -u origin main

#After changes
git add .
git commit -m "updated project"
git push

#20. If Local Branch and Remote Branch Names Are Different

#Example error:

#upstream branch does not match current branch

#Fix:

git push -u origin main

or if remote branch is master:

git push origin HEAD:master

#Better option:

git branch -M main
git push -u origin main

#21. Git Ignore

Create .gitignore file:

touch .gitignore

#Example .gitignore:

node_modules/
.env
dist/
build/
*.log

#Then:

git add .gitignore
git commit -m "added gitignore"

#22. Stash Commands

#Stash temporarily saves uncommitted changes.

Save changes
git stash
View stash list
git stash list
Apply latest stash
git stash apply
Apply and remove stash
git stash pop
Delete stash
git stash drop

#23. Tags

#Tags are used for releases.

git tag v1.0
git push origin v1.0

View tags:

git tag

#24. Important Interview Explanation
#Working Directory

#Where you edit files.

#Staging Area

#Where files are prepared before commit.

#Local Repository

#Where commits are stored locally.

#Remote Repository

#GitHub/GitLab/Bitbucket online repo.

#Flow:

#Working Directory → Staging Area → Local Repo → Remote Repo

#Commands:

git add .
git commit -m "message"
git push

#25. Most Used Git Commands Table

#Command	Use
git init	Create local Git repo
git status	Check file status
git add .	Stage all changes
git commit -m "msg"	Save changes
git log --oneline	View commit history
git branch	Show branches
git checkout -b branch	Create and switch branch
git merge branch	Merge branch
git remote -v	Check remote URL
git push	Upload code
git pull	Download latest changes
git clone URL	Copy remote repo
git stash	Temporarily save changes
git reset	Undo commits

#26. Daily Git Practice Script

#Use this every day:

git status
git add .
git commit -m "meaningful commit message"
git pull origin main
git push origin main

#For feature branch:

git checkout -b feature-name
git add .
git commit -m "added feature"
git push origin feature-name

#27. Best Commit Message Examples

#Good:

git commit -m "added login page"
git commit -m "fixed navbar alignment"
git commit -m "updated Dockerfile"
git commit -m "configured GitHub Actions workflow"

#Bad:

git commit -m "changes"
git commit -m "update"
git commit -m "final"

#28. Important DevOps Git Commands

#Clone application repo
git clone https://github.com/user/app.git

#Checkout deployment branch
git checkout dev
Pull latest code before build
git pull origin dev

#Create release tag
git tag v1.0.0
git push origin v1.0.0

#Check latest commit
git log --oneline -5

